(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-axial-skeleton-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"The Axial Skeleton — Anatomy Exam 4 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How many total bones make up the adult vertebral column, and how are they distributed?","choices":["26 bones: 24 vertebrae, 1 sacrum, and 1 coccyx","33 bones: 24 vertebrae, 5 sacral, and 4 coccygeal, all unfused","26 bones: 26 individual vertebrae with no fused segments","24 bones: 24 vertebrae only, with no sacrum or coccyx counted"],"answer":0,"exp":"The adult vertebral column is made up of 26 bones: 24 vertebrae (7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar), 1 sacrum (5 fused vertebrae), and 1 coccyx (3 to 5 fused vertebrae). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How many cervical, thoracic, and lumbar vertebrae are there, respectively?","choices":["7 cervical, 5 thoracic, 12 lumbar","7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar","5 cervical, 12 thoracic, 7 lumbar","12 cervical, 7 thoracic, 5 lumbar"],"answer":1,"exp":"There are 7 cervical vertebrae, 12 thoracic vertebrae, and 5 lumbar vertebrae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which of the following is a listed function of the vertebral column?","choices":["Encloses and protects the spinal cord while supporting the skull and transferring weight to the lower limbs","Serves as the body's exclusive, primary site for all red blood cell production, independent of other bones","Forms the main structural framework that is directly responsible for respiration and movement of the rib cage","Houses and protects the pituitary gland within a small, dedicated bony recess of the skull"],"answer":0,"exp":"The vertebral column encloses and protects the spinal cord, supports the skull and the weight of the head/neck/trunk, transfers weight to the lower limbs, and helps maintain upright posture. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"How many major curves does the adult vertebral column have, and what are they?","choices":["5: cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal curves","4: cervical, thoracic, lumbar, and sacral curves","3: cervical, thoracic, and lumbar curves only","2: an upper curve and a lower curve"],"answer":1,"exp":"There are 4 major curves of the vertebral column: the cervical curve, thoracic curve, lumbar curve, and sacral curve. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What distinguishes a primary spinal curve from a secondary spinal curve?","choices":["Primary curves develop before birth; secondary curves develop after birth","Primary curves occur only in the cervical region; secondary only in the lumbar region","Primary curves develop after birth; secondary curves develop before birth","There is no developmental difference between them"],"answer":0,"exp":"Primary curves develop before birth, while secondary curves develop after birth (such as the cervical curve developing as an infant learns to balance its head, and the lumbar curve developing with the ability to stand). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"What separates one vertebral body from the next, and what is it made of?","choices":["The intervertebral disc, a pad of cartilage","The vertebral arch, made entirely of bone","The pedicle, made of dense ligament tissue","The lamina, made of hyaline cartilage"],"answer":0,"exp":"A vertebral body is separated from the adjacent vertebral body by the intervertebral disc, a pad of cartilage. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What does the vertebral arch form, and what is it composed of?","choices":["The transverse foramen; composed of the lamina only","The vertebral foramen; composed of the pedicle and laminae","The intervertebral disc; composed of the pedicle only","The vertebral body; composed entirely of cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vertebral arch forms the vertebral foramen and is made of the pedicle and laminae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What is spina bifida, per the clinical note in the material?","choices":["Fusion of two or more adjacent vertebral bodies together","A traumatic fracture involving the vertebral body itself","A malformation where the vertebral arch fails to close completely","Traumatic dislocation of the atlas from the occipital condyles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Spina bifida is a malformation of the structures making up the vertebral arch, where the vertebral arch fails to close completely. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which processes of the vertebra allow for articulation with adjacent vertebrae above and below?","choices":["Pedicle and lamina of the vertebral arch","Superior and inferior articular processes","Costal facets present on thoracic vertebrae","Spinous and transverse processes"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior and inferior articular processes are the structures that allow articulation with adjacent vertebrae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which feature is characteristic of all cervical vertebrae?","choices":["Fusion with adjacent vertebrae","A transverse foramen in each transverse process","A costal facet for rib articulation","A large, block-shaped vertebral body with a posteriorly pointed spinous process"],"answer":1,"exp":"All cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina, and their transverse processes are fused to the costal processes. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which cervical vertebra lacks a bifid spinous process, unlike the others?","choices":["C3","C7","C5","C1 (atlas)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Spinous processes of the cervical vertebrae are bifid except for C7, which instead has one long, prominent, non-bifid spinous process. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What is distinctive about the atlas (C1) compared to typical vertebrae, and what movement does it allow?","choices":["It has a dens that projects superiorly from its body; it allows lateral flexion of the neck","It is fused directly to the axis below it, which allows essentially no independent movement","It has no body and instead consists of anterior/posterior arches and tubercles; it allows the head to nod \"yes\"","It has the largest vertebral body of any cervical vertebra; it allows the head to rotate \"no\""],"answer":2,"exp":"The atlas (C1) has no body, instead consisting of anterior and posterior vertebral arches and tubercles, and it articulates with the occipital condyles to allow the head to nod in a \"yes\" motion. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What structure is unique to the axis (C2), and what does it allow?","choices":["The transverse foramen, allowing passage of the vertebral artery on both sides","The bifid spinous process, permitting forward flexion of the cervical spine","The costal facet, allowing direct articulation with the first pair of ribs","The dens, bound to the atlas by the transverse ligament, allowing the head to move in a \"no\" manner"],"answer":3,"exp":"The axis (C2) has a dens, which is bound to the atlas by the transverse ligament, allowing the head to pivot in a \"no\" motion. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Why is C7 called the \"vertebra prominens,\" and what ligament attaches there?","choices":["It lacks a spinous process entirely, and the anterior longitudinal ligament attaches there instead","It has the largest vertebral foramen of any cervical vertebra, and the transverse ligament attaches there","It has a long, prominent spinous process where the ligamentum nuchae begins, extending to the external occipital crest","It is the only cervical vertebra bearing true costal facets, and the supraspinous ligament attaches there"],"answer":2,"exp":"C7 is called the vertebra prominens because it has a long, prominent spinous process; the ligamentum nuchae, a large elastic ligament, begins there and extends to the external occipital crest of the skull. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What clinical condition involving sudden head motion and vertebral damage is noted in association with the cervical spine?","choices":["Herniated disc","Sciatica","Whiplash","Spina bifida"],"answer":2,"exp":"Whiplash is the clinical application noted — a sudden motion of the head resulting in vertebral damage. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"How many thoracic vertebrae are there, and what unique feature do they have?","choices":["12; they have transverse foramina like cervical vertebrae","7; they have the largest vertebral bodies of the spine","12; they have costal facets for rib articulation","5; they lack articular processes"],"answer":2,"exp":"There are 12 thoracic vertebrae, and they have costal facets that allow them to articulate with the ribs. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"How do the costal facets of T1–T8 differ from those of T9–T12?","choices":["T1–T8 have superior and inferior costal facets, while T9–T12 have only one facet","There is no meaningful difference between the two groups of thoracic vertebrae","T1–T8 have no costal facets at all, while T9–T12 have two facets each","T1–T8 have transverse foramina, while T9–T12 have costal facets instead"],"answer":0,"exp":"T1 to T8 have both superior and inferior costal facets, while T9 to T12 have only one facet each. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"How many lumbar vertebrae are there, and what is a distinguishing feature of their spinous processes?","choices":["12; the spinous process points inferiorly","5; the spinous process points posteriorly","7; the spinous process points laterally","5; the spinous process is bifid"],"answer":1,"exp":"There are 5 lumbar vertebrae, and their spinous process points posteriorly (rather than inferiorly as in most thoracic vertebrae, or being bifid as in most cervical vertebrae). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What is a distinguishing feature of lumbar vertebral bodies compared to other regions, consistent with their function?","choices":["They contain transverse foramina for the vertebral arteries","They are quite large, supporting the weight of the torso","They are absent, replaced entirely by the vertebral arch","They are the smallest of all vertebral bodies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lumbar vertebral bodies are quite large, reflecting their function of supporting the weight of the torso. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"How many originally separate vertebrae fuse to form the adult sacrum?","choices":["Twelve","Three","Five","Seven"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sacrum consists of five fused vertebrae, though it exists as one bone in the adult. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What is the sacral promontory?","choices":["An opening for passage of the cauda equina, located at the sacral apex","A fibrous ligament connecting the sacrum directly to the coccyx below it","A synovial joint formed between the sacrum and the adjacent ilium","A structure of the sacrum, part of its listed anatomical landmarks near the base"],"answer":3,"exp":"The sacral promontory is one of the anatomical structures of the sacrum, located near its base. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"What is the auricular surface of the sacrum, and what does it articulate with?","choices":["A surface that forms part of the sacro-iliac joint, articulating with the ilium","The opening through which nerve roots first enter the sacral canal","A landmark serving as the attachment point for the ligamentum nuchae","A smooth surface for articulation between the sacrum and the coccyx"],"answer":0,"exp":"The auricular surface is a structure of the sacrum that forms the sacro-iliac joint by articulating with the ilium of the hip bone. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"How many fused vertebrae typically make up the coccyx?","choices":["Six or seven","Three to five","Exactly five","Exactly two"],"answer":1,"exp":"The coccyx consists of three to five fused vertebrae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"How does the orientation of the adult coccyx typically differ between males and females?","choices":["Both point posteriorly with no sex difference","Both point anteriorly with no sex difference","The adult male coccyx points inferiorly, while the adult female coccyx points anteriorly","The adult male coccyx points anteriorly, while the adult female coccyx points inferiorly"],"answer":3,"exp":"The adult male coccyx typically points anteriorly, while the adult female coccyx typically points inferiorly. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What are the two structural functions of the thoracic cage listed in the material?","choices":["Protecting thoracic organs and serving as an attachment site for respiration/posture/limb-movement muscles","Forming the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone and enclosing the pituitary gland","Producing red blood cells within its marrow and storing the body's calcium reserves","Supporting the skull directly and transferring the body's weight down to the lower limbs"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thoracic cage protects the heart, lungs, thymus, and other structures within the cavity, and serves as an attachment site for muscles involved in respiration, positioning the vertebral column, and movements of the pectoral girdle and upper limb. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Which ribs are classified as \"true ribs,\" and which as \"false ribs\"?","choices":["True ribs are 1–7; false ribs are 8–12","True ribs are 1–12; there are no false ribs","True ribs are 8–12; false ribs are 1–7","True ribs are 1–10; false ribs are 11–12"],"answer":0,"exp":"True ribs are ribs 1–7, and false ribs are ribs 8–12. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What distinguishes vertebrochondral ribs from vertebrosternal ribs?","choices":["Vertebrochondral ribs (8-10) connect to the sternum indirectly via shared costal cartilage, while vertebrosternal ribs (1-7) connect directly via their own costal cartilage","There is no real difference between them; the two terms are simply used interchangeably by clinicians and anatomists alike","Vertebrochondral ribs (1-7) connect directly to the sternum, while vertebrosternal ribs (8-10) do not connect to the sternum at all","Vertebrochondral ribs possess no costal cartilage whatsoever, while vertebrosternal ribs are made entirely of cartilage instead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vertebrosternal ribs (1–7) connect to the sternum via their own costal cartilages, while vertebrochondral ribs (8–10) connect to the sternum indirectly, via costal cartilage shared with the rib above. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Why are ribs 11 and 12 called \"floating ribs\"?","choices":["They are the only ribs without a head, neck, and body","They articulate with two thoracic vertebrae instead of one","They lack tubercles","They have no anterior cartilage attachment to the sternum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ribs 11 and 12 are called floating (vertebral) ribs because they have no anterior costal cartilage attaching them to the sternum. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What three parts make up the sternum?","choices":["Manubrium, body, and xiphoid process","Manubrium, jugular notch, and body only","Manubrium, xiphoid process, and costal cartilage","Body, xiphoid process, and clavicular notch"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sternum consists of the manubrium, body, and xiphoid process. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Where is the jugular notch located, and what is its significance?","choices":["On the manubrium of the sternum, a palpable landmark at its superior border","Between the sacrum and the coccyx, at the base of the spine","On the body of the sternum, where ribs two through seven attach","On the xiphoid process, marking the sternum's inferior tip"],"answer":0,"exp":"The jugular notch is located on the manubrium of the sternum, at its superior border, near the clavicular articulation. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 100"}]};
